\title{Euclidean Algorithm (simple version)}
\date{2026-07-14T17:49:05Z}
\tag{discrete}
\taxon{Definition}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{We can put these observations about #{\gcd} together to write down a procedure for computing it.  This is one of the oldest recorded algorithms, and is named the \em{Euclidean Algorithm} since it is in Euclid's \em{Elements}, written in about 300 BC.  It was also independently invented in both India and China.  This simple version is essentially equivalent to the version found in the \em{Elements}, although we will also describe a slightly more efficient version later.}

\ul{
  \li{#{\gcd(a,0) = a}.}
  \li{If #{a \geq b}, then #{\gcd(a,b) = \gcd(a-b,b)}.}
  \li{Otherwise, #{\gcd(a,b) = \gcd(b,a)}.}
}

\p{Each of these steps is justified by one of our previous theorems,
and #{a} and #{b} keep getting smaller and smaller so #{b} must eventually reach #{0}.}
